% ============================================================================
% body-counts — how many of each major structure the human body has
% (adj-facts-stdlib, ANATOMY).
% ============================================================================
% An anatomy facts library in the ADJ standard library — the kind a MEDICINE
% agent `import`s when it needs a grounded, citable structural fact about the
% human body: "an adult skeleton has 206 bones," "the heart has 4 chambers," "a
% human cell carries 46 chromosomes." These are the counts a clinician reasons
% FROM — a rib-fracture note that reads "fracture of the 13th rib" is suspect
% because there are only twelve pairs; a karyotype with 47 chromosomes is a
% trisomy precisely because the normal count is 46. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? body_count(heart_chambers, $N)      % 4
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `body_count(structure, count)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the number
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a
% structure not in the table (it never invents a count). The query also runs
% BACKWARD — bind a count and recall the structure(s) that have it:
%
%     ? body_count($S, 206)                 % bones_in_adult_body
%
% Because each `count` value is a NUMBER (not a label), a recalled count
% composes straight into arithmetic and constraint checks: `pairs_of_ribs` (12)
% doubles to 24 individual ribs; an observed chromosome count can be compared to
% 46 to flag aneuploidy — the concrete bridge from anatomy RECALL to COMPUTE.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every count below was read
% out of a fetched authoritative page, and any count that could not be verified
% there would have been dropped). Each structure→count pair, with the primary
% U.S. government / NIH source it was copied from and the verbatim span that
% states it:
%
%   chromosomes → 46
%     https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Chromosome  (NIH / NHGRI)
%     "Humans have 22 pairs of numbered chromosomes (autosomes) and one pair
%      of sex chromosomes (XX or XY), for a total of 46."
%
%   bones_in_adult_body → 206
%     https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537199/  (NIH / NLM, StatPearls)
%     "Human infants typically have 270 bones, fusing into around 206 in the
%      human adult."
%
%   heart_chambers → 4
%     https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/heart/anatomy  (NIH / NHLBI)
%     "It has four hollow chambers surrounded by muscle and other heart tissue."
%
%   lungs → 2
%     https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/lungs  (NIH / NHLBI)
%     "the pair of spongy, pinkish-gray organs in your chest"
%
%   pairs_of_ribs → 12
%     https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538328/  (NIH / NLM, StatPearls)
%     "Generally, there are twelve pairs of ribs."
%
%   kidneys → 2
%     https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/urinary/components/kidney.html
%     (NIH / NCI SEER Training)
%     "The paired kidneys are located between the twelfth thoracic and third
%      lumbar vertebrae, one on each side of the vertebral column."
%
% EXTENDED this cycle with the three hand-bone GROUP counts. These reuse the
% SAME already-quoted, already-cited NCBI-Bookshelf sentence that
% `hand-bones.adj` (`hand_bone_region(group, region)`) already ships in its
% own header -- no new WebFetch. `hand_bone_region` decodes WHERE each group
% sits; this table decodes HOW MANY bones are in each group, a number the
% source states plainly alongside the location:
%
%   carpals → 8
%   metacarpals → 5
%   phalanges → 14
%     https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279362/
%     (InformedHealth.org, "In brief: How do hands work?" — IQWiG teaching
%      summary hosted on the U.S. NIH / NCBI Bookshelf)
%     "The human hand is made up of a total of 27 individual bones: 8 carpal
%      bones (in the base of the hand), 5 metacarpal bones (in the middle
%      part of the hand) and 14 phalanges (finger bones) are connected by
%      joints and ligaments."
%   Unlike the other six rows, this source is a patient-education / teaching
%   summary (IQWiG), not a primary government anatomy authority — so these
%   three rows are honestly `consensus`-tier, one rung below the table's
%   `authoritative` envelope trust, exactly as `hand-bones.adj` itself
%   already documents for the same sentence.
%
% EVERY ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SPAN, ITS OWN LOCATOR, AND — where it differs —
% ITS OWN TIER, below, in the row's `{ ... }` block (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e). Nine
% rows, seven pages.
%
% WHAT #14986 CHANGED HERE, and this table's defect is sharper than its
% siblings'. The paragraph that stood here read: "An ADJ `table` carries ONE
% provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/`trust` below hold the single
% strongest span: the explicitly numeric NHGRI statement that fixes the first
% row (chromosomes → 46). Every OTHER row's per-fact source, verbatim span, and
% real trust tier is listed above, so each count remains independently
% checkable. Six of the nine sources are primary U.S. government / NIH
% references (`trust authoritative`); the three hand-bone-group rows are
% `consensus`-tier per their own IQWiG source, as noted above."
%
% Two defects in one paragraph:
%
%   1. THE SPAN. `source` is the field that carries the tier, so all nine rows
%      were warranted by a sentence that counts CHROMOSOMES. A recall of
%      `pairs_of_ribs` came back proved by "Humans have 22 pairs of numbered
%      chromosomes ... for a total of 46."
%
%   2. THE TIER, and this one the file DECLARED and then contradicted. It says
%      plainly that the three hand-bone rows "are `consensus`-tier, one rung
%      below the table's `authoritative` envelope trust" — and shipped
%      `authoritative` on them, because one envelope imposes one tier. The e2e
%      test that queried exactly those three rows asserted only their NUMBERS,
%      so nothing caught it. They now carry `trust consensus` in the row, which
%      is what a query returns.
%
% TWO FACTS ABOUT ROW/ENVELOPE PROVENANCE, ESTABLISHED BY RUNNING IT rather
% than by reading the lowering code:
%
%   * a row's `trust` DOES override the envelope's, per row;
%   * an envelope `source` is REQUIRED — an envelope carrying only `locator`
%     and `trust` is rejected with `Lower(TableMissingProvenance)`. The framing
%     span below is not a stylistic choice.
%
% THE ENVELOPE IS NOW A FRAMING SPAN: the SEER module's opening description of
% the body as billions of smaller structures. It states no count of anything
% this table counts, so it warrants none of the nine rows. It ends on the
% page's own colon, where that sentence hands off to the list of the four
% kinds — the page's boundary, not a cut.
%
% ONE SPAN WAS WIDENED. The lungs row quoted "the pair of spongy, pinkish-gray
% organs in your chest" — opening mid-sentence, so the quoted run never said
% whose, or that it was about lungs at all. The page's full sentence is
% contiguous: "Your lungs are the pair of spongy, pinkish-gray organs in your
% chest." Same class as the truncation triage in #15185.
%
% THREE ROWS SHARE THE HAND SENTENCE, so each carries its own copy and each is
% pinned individually: one broken copy behind two intact ones is the failure
% `biology/skeleton-bones.adj` shipped.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table body_count {
    columns structure, count

    % Each row carries the sentence that STATES ITS COUNT, that
    % sentence's page, and — for the three hand-bone rows — its own
    % TIER. Until #14986 one envelope imposed the chromosome sentence
    % and `trust authoritative` on all nine, while this file's header
    % already said the hand-bone rows are honestly `consensus`.
    row (chromosomes, 46) {
        source "Humans have 22 pairs of numbered chromosomes (autosomes) and one pair of sex chromosomes (XX or XY), for a total of 46."
        locator "https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Chromosome"
    }
    row (bones_in_adult_body, 206) {
        source "Human infants typically have 270 bones, fusing into around 206 in the human adult."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537199/"
    }
    row (heart_chambers, 4) {
        source "It has four hollow chambers surrounded by muscle and other heart tissue."
        locator "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/heart/anatomy"
    }
    row (lungs, 2) {
        source "Your lungs are the pair of spongy, pinkish-gray organs in your chest."
        locator "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/lungs"
    }
    row (pairs_of_ribs, 12) {
        source "Generally, there are twelve pairs of ribs."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538328/"
    }
    row (kidneys, 2) {
        source "The paired kidneys are located between the twelfth thoracic and third lumbar vertebrae, one on each side of the vertebral column."
        locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/urinary/components/kidney.html"
    }
    row (carpals, 8) {
        source "The human hand is made up of a total of 27 individual bones: 8 carpal bones (in the base of the hand), 5 metacarpal bones (in the middle part of the hand) and 14 phalanges (finger bones) are connected by joints and ligaments."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279362/"
        trust consensus
    }
    row (metacarpals, 5) {
        source "The human hand is made up of a total of 27 individual bones: 8 carpal bones (in the base of the hand), 5 metacarpal bones (in the middle part of the hand) and 14 phalanges (finger bones) are connected by joints and ligaments."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279362/"
        trust consensus
    }
    row (phalanges, 14) {
        source "The human hand is made up of a total of 27 individual bones: 8 carpal bones (in the base of the hand), 5 metacarpal bones (in the middle part of the hand) and 14 phalanges (finger bones) are connected by joints and ligaments."
        locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279362/"
        trust consensus
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE IS A FRAMING SPAN: the SEER module's opening
    % description of the body as many smaller structures. It states no
    % COUNT of anything this table counts, so it warrants none of the nine
    % rows. It ends on the page's own colon, where the sentence hands off
    % to the list of the four kinds — the page's boundary, not a cut.
    %
    % An envelope `source` is REQUIRED, established by running it: an
    % envelope with only `locator` and `trust` is rejected with
    % `Lower(TableMissingProvenance)`. The framing span is not a style
    % choice.
    source "The human body is a single structure but it is made up of billions of smaller structures of four major kinds:"
    locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/body/"
    trust authoritative
}
